  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-Atlantic \nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period of \nbreezy to strong NE winds through early next week with \nstrongest winds along the coast, especially south of Cape \nHatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, combined with \nupcoming King Tide cycle lead to additional minor coastal \nimpacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).\n\nFor Hatteras Island, seas offshore have increased yet again to\n8-10 ft, and these higher seas will continue today, and only\nvery slowly subside through the coming week, and likely remain\nabove 6 ft through mid week. Guidance available in our system\ncontinues to be underdone so went with NBM (highest available)\nand manually bumped up values for waters N of Cape Hatteras. This\nwill continue to bring the threat of ocean overwash to \nvulnerable areas where the dune structure has been compromised, \nespecially around the times of high tide. For ocean-side: high \ntide is between 1830-1930edt this evening, and 0700-0800edt SUN.\nSoundside high tides lag from 2 to 3 hours behind the oceanside\ntides. The National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported \nsubstantial beach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along \nwith significant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe.\nDue to hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains \nclosed from the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. \n\nThe region is also entering a King Tide cycle this weekend that will \nbuild and peak early to mid next week. With a trend in higher\nseas coinciding with the building tides next week, coastal\nflooding will be likely well into next week. Even with winds\ndiminishing by mid week, \"sunny day\" coastal flooding across \nmuch of the tidal regions of ENC around times of high tide will\ncontinue through mid week. The areas most susceptible to street\nflooding will be low lying areas along the Neuse and Bay rivers\nas well as Downeast Carteret County to North Topsail Beach which\nhas already been seen during the last two morning's high tides.\nThe morning high tide will be the highest of the daily tides \nthis weekend and next week with the King Tide peaking TUE and \nWED.\n\nHave extended the coastal flood advisories into WED afternoon, \nwith King Tides lasting through mid week. There is still\npotential that the headlines for TUE and WED may need to be \nupgraded to coastal flood warnings. Will wait for this decision \nafter we see what SUN and MON bring. In addition, if seas \nincrease any further than currently forecast, a period of \ncoastal flood warnings may be needed for parts of the OBX, and \nwill need to monitor trends.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...POckets of moisture continue to be squeezed\nbetween the inland high and offshore low. Though almost all\nguidance keeps the bulk of precip activity off the coast and\nperhaps skirting OBX, some light returns are evident on KMHX\nradar this afternoon. Have added a 15-19% chance for portions \nof the Inner Banks this afternoon into this evening. Pleasantly \ncooler and drier air mass will remain with us through much of \nthe extended period with near average highs, to slightly below \navg lows, esp mid week when high pres builds and good \nradiational cooling yet again sets up with lows well down into \nthe 40s with some 30s again possible. Maximum temperatures \nthrough the period are expected to remain in the mid 60s (OBX) \nto lower 70s range.",
